## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about crash safety, non-volatile memory semantics, and low-level ordering. Requires explicit use of durable writes, recovery procedures, and a practical benchmark plan.

## Key Concepts

- Crash-consistency and redo-logs
- Durable writes: clwb/flushopt, sfence
- Per-slot sequencing and tail/head management
- Recovery replay and correctness guarantees

## Code Example

```pseudo
structure Slot { payload; header; seq; }
function Enqueue(buf, item): void {
  Slot s = select_next_slot();
  s.payload = item;
  s.header = make_header(++tail_seq);
  persist(s.header); // clwb/flushopt + sfence
  fence();
  tail = tail + 1; // release store
}
function Dequeue(): Item {
  if (head == tail) return EMPTY;
  Item it = read_slot(head);
  head = head + 1; // release
  persist(head);
  return it;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale this across NUMA nodes?
- How would you test resilience against partial writes or power loss during enqueue?

## Why This Is Asked

The task probes practical memory allocator design under NUMA, highlighting locality, contention, and fragmentation trade-offs with lock-free data structures.

## Key Concepts

- NUMA locality
- Per-thread caches
- Lock-free freelists and memory reclamation
- Fragmentation and refill strategies
- Benchmark design

## Code Example

```c
// High-level sketch of data structures
typedef struct Block { struct Block *next; } Block;
typedef struct { _Atomic(Block*) central; Block *local; int node; } Allocator;

void* malloc64(Allocator *A){
  Block *b = A->local; if(b){ A->local = b->next; return (void*)b; }
  Block *c = atomic_load_explicit(&A->central, memory_order_acquire);
  if(c){ atomic_store_explicit(&A->central, c->next, memory_order_release); return (void*)c; }
  // refill from OS or malloc from system
  return system_alloc(A);
}
void free64(Allocator *A, Block *b){
  b->next = A->local; A->local = b;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle thread migration across NUMA nodes? 
- What metrics identify improvement (latency, bandwidth, cache misses)?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests crash‑consistency and lock‑free design under PMEM constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Crash‑consistency with persistent memory using CAS, fences, and transactional updates
- Per‑thread caches with a central allocator to reduce cross‑socket traffic
- Recovery and durability: reconstruct free lists from persistent root and/or logs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: pmem_alloc
function pmem_alloc() {
  if (!threadCache.empty()) return threadCache.pop();
  let batch = centralPool.acquireBatch();
  threadCache.fill(batch);
  return threadCache.pop();
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests basic concurrent data structure design and practical use of atomics and memory ordering in a beginner-friendly but realistic setting.

## Key Concepts
- MPSC synchronization using a shared tail and a local consumer head
- Atomic fetch_add for slot reservation
- Memory ordering: release on enqueue, acquire on dequeue
- Correct wrap-around handling in a fixed-size buffer

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-C code (C11 atomics style)
#define N (1<<12)
typedef struct { uint64_t data; uint64_t seq; } slot_t;
slot_t buf[N];
atomic_uintptr_t tail = 0; // producer index
size_t head = 0; // consumer index

bool enqueue(uint64_t v){
  size_t t = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  if (t >= N) return false;
  buf[t].data = v;
  atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
  buf[t].seq = t; // simple wrap-safe signal
  return true;
}

bool dequeue(uint64_t* out){
  if (head >= atomic_load_explicit(&tail, memory_order_acquire)) return false;
  *out = buf[head].data;
  head++;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to detect and recover from corrupted data?
- How would you benchmark throughput under 1000 producers vs 1 consumer?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests durability and cross-process coordination under crash scenarios using low-level memory hygiene.

## Key Concepts
- Durable memory ordering with CLWB/CLFLUSHOPT and sfence
- Per-slot sequencing to avoid ABA
- Shared memory persistence across crashes
- Recovery algorithm from persistent metadata

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal sketch
typedef struct { uint64_t seq; char data[256]; } slot_t;
typedef struct { slot_t slots[N]; uint64_t head, tail; } ring_t;

uint64_t pos = __sync_fetch_and_add(&ring->tail, 1);
slot_t *s = &ring->slots[pos % N];
while (s->seq != pos) {}
memcpy(s->data, payload, len);
persist(&s->data, len);
__sync_synchronize();
s->seq = pos + 1;
persist(&s->seq, sizeof(uint64_t));
```

