- How would you adapt for variable-sized payloads?
- How would you test durability under power loss and crash scenarios?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of per-thread buffering, cache-line alignment, and atomic merges to minimize contention in high-frequency workloads.

## Key Concepts
- Thread-local buffers to avoid contention
- Cache-line alignment to prevent false sharing
- Atomic merges with memory_order_relaxed for performance

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdalign.h>
#define NBINS 256

typedef struct {
  alignas(64) unsigned long long bins[NBINS];
} thread_hist_t;

unsigned long long global_bins[NBINS];

void add_sample(thread_hist_t* t, unsigned int v) {
  t->bins[v]++;
}

void merge_all_to_global(thread_hist_t* t) {
  for (int i = 0; i < NBINS; ++i)
    __atomic_fetch_add(&global_bins[i], t->bins[i], __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
  // optionally clear t->bins here
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for dynamic bin counts or streaming merges?
- What profiling steps would you take to quantify false-sharing effects and contention under multi-threaded load?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of real-world low-latency data structures, focusing on lock-free progress, ABA avoidance, and safe resizing in a multi-core, multi-socket environment. The map should support rapid updates to routing or state tables with minimal stalls.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free design and correctness under high contention
- ABA avoidance via per-bucket sequence counters
- Open addressing with linear probing
- Precise memory ordering and fences for concurrent updates
- NUMA-aware bucket allocation to reduce cross-socket memory traffic
- Safe, incremental resizing and slot reclamation

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
  uint64_t key;
  uint64_t value;
  uint32_t seq;        // sequence counter
  uint8_t  state;      // 0=empty, 1=filled, 2=tombstone
} bucket_t;

bucket_t* table = aligned_alloc(64, N * sizeof(bucket_t));

// PUT: lock-free insert with CAS
bool put(uint64_t key, uint64_t value) {
  uint32_t h = hash(key) & (N-1);
  for (int i = 0; i < MAX_PROBE; ++i) {
    bucket_t* b = &table[(h + i) & (N-1)];
    uint32_t seq = b->seq;
    if (b->state == 0) {  // empty
      bucket_t new = {key, value, seq, 1};
      if (__sync_bool_compare_and_swap(b, &new, sizeof(bucket_t))) {
        return true;
      }
    }
  }
  return false;
}

// GET: validate sequence to avoid ABA
uint64_t get(uint64_t key) {
  uint32_t h = hash(key) & (N-1);
  for (int i = 0; i < MAX_PROBE; ++i) {
    bucket_t* b = &table[(h + i) & (N-1)];
    if (b->state == 0) return NOT_FOUND;
    if (b->state == 1 && b->key == key) {
      // re-check sequence for ABA safety
      if (b->seq == expected_seq) return b->value;
    }
  }
  return NOT_FOUND;
}
```
